From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Simon Elsbrock <simon@iodev.org>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 11:30:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] mtd: nandx: add the sub-page write option

The nandx port tests CONFIG_MTK_NANDX_WRITE_SUBPAGE, but no Kconfig defines
it, so it is always off and the driver reports a 4096-byte sub-page. The Echo
Dot (5th gen) volumes were written with 2048-byte sub-pages: Amazon's U-Boot
reports "sub-page size 2048" and a VID header offset of 2048. With 4096 the
kernel found the EC and VID headers in one minimal I/O unit, attached UBI
read-only, and UBIFS could not mount the userdata volume read-write (measured
on a C2N6L4, 2026-11-26). Amazon's 5.4 kernel defines the option in
drivers/mtd/Kconfig and its mt8519_defconfig sets it.

---
--- a/drivers/mtd/nandx/Kconfig
+++ b/drivers/mtd/nandx/Kconfig
@@ -3,3 +3,13 @@
 	depends on MTD && (ARCH_MEDIATEK || COMPILE_TEST)
 	help
 	  MediaTek's nandx SPI-NAND driver for the MT8519.
+
+config MTK_NANDX_WRITE_SUBPAGE
+	bool "MediaTek MT8519 NAND sub-page writes"
+	depends on MTD_NANDX_MT8519
+	help
+	  Program the two halves of a NAND page separately, as Amazon's
+	  bootloader and kernel do. UBI then keeps a block's erase-counter and
+	  volume headers in the two halves of its first page, the layout the
+	  Echo Dot (5th gen) volumes were written with. Without it UBI attaches
+	  them read-only.
